# NEGATIVE CONTROL FIXTURE -- deliberately broken, and it must stay broken.
#
# Not a `.rule` file, and the extension is load-bearing: run-scenario.sh globs
# `$SCENARIO/*.rule` when a scenario has no driver, and this scenario HAS a
# driver, but a future edit that removed driver.sh would otherwise silently
# fold a fixture built to FAIL into the scenario's honest assertions.
# driver.sh names this file explicitly.
#
# WHAT IT PROVES. The `fanout` coverage oracle is the load-bearing assertion of
# the whole cross-process shm lens: it is what stops the lens passing having
# sampled a single worker N times. An oracle nobody has watched fail is an
# oracle nobody should trust, so driver.sh runs this fixture against the same
# live four-worker server the honest cases ran against, and requires it to come
# back RED with the coverage message.
#
# HOW THE FAILURE IS FORCED. `fanout 8 8` demands 8 DISTINCT answering workers
# from a server that has exactly 4. That is unreachable by construction, not by
# luck: no scheduling outcome, no accept-path behaviour and no load level can
# produce a ninth worker, so this fixture is deterministically red on every box
# and cannot flake green. Compare the alternatives, both rejected:
#
#   - `fanout 2 2` (hope both legs land on one worker) is red only
#     PROBABILISTICALLY -- it would flake green, and a negative control that
#     sometimes passes is worse than none, because it teaches the reader the
#     oracle fired when it did not.
#   - a second single-worker server would be a truer mirror of the real
#     failure, but a scenario boots ONE server; forcing coverage to be
#     unreachable from the other side of the same inequality tests the same
#     comparison, in the same code, with no second process.
#
# It exercises the ceiling side of `distinct < min_workers` rather than the
# floor side the field reports naturally, and the comparison it reddens is the
# single one in eval_fanout_coverage. The floor side was ALSO observed red
# unmutated on this box while sizing the honest cases: `fanout 8` with
# min_workers 3 returned "8 of 8 responses came from 2 distinct workers, need
# >= 3" on four consecutive runs. Both directions of the same bound have been
# seen to fail.
#
# DO NOT "FIX" THIS FILE. A red here is the pass condition. If it ever comes
# back green, the coverage oracle stopped comparing and every fanout in the
# tree became a test that cannot fail.
name    NEGATIVE CONTROL: coverage the server cannot reach must FAIL
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  body~OK
pid_may_change
fanout  8 8
